1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,865
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,865 --> 00:00:08,451
This is chapter thirty-seven: profiling with Java Flight Recorder.

3
00:00:08,901 --> 00:00:12,626
Most performance problems can't be reproduced locally.

4
00:00:12,626 --> 00:00:15,800
The data, the load and the heap are different.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:23,941
Flight Recorder is a profiler built into the JVM, cheap enough to leave running, so it records what actually happened.

6
00:00:24,841 --> 00:00:26,732
Here's the classic incident.

7
00:00:26,732 --> 00:00:37,066
Latency spiked to two point four seconds at two minutes past two, recovered on its own by nine past, and at eleven past someone asks you to look into it.

8
00:00:37,066 --> 00:00:44,361
You have graphs showing that it happened, logs showing nothing unusual, and a service now behaving normally.

9
00:00:44,811 --> 00:00:49,291
Reproducing locally won't work, because the load and data are different.

10
00:00:49,741 --> 00:00:54,071
Adding logging gives you nothing until it happens again, maybe next month.

11
00:00:54,521 --> 00:00:58,063
Attaching a profiler means being attached when it happened.

12
00:00:58,513 --> 00:01:05,659
What you need is a recording that was already running, detailed enough to answer questions you hadn't thought to ask yet.

13
00:01:06,559 --> 00:01:19,466
The JVM and JDK libraries are already instrumented with hundreds of event types: GC phases, lock contention, socket reads, class loading, allocations, exceptions.

14
00:01:19,916 --> 00:01:24,823
JFR writes the enabled ones into per-thread buffers, with no locking.

15
00:01:25,273 --> 00:01:28,067
Full buffers are handed to a global buffer,

16
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:31,269
and a background thread writes chunks to disk.

17
00:01:31,719 --> 00:01:35,976
So the overhead is about one percent with the default settings.

18
00:01:35,976 --> 00:01:38,679
And it records meaning, not just stacks.

19
00:01:38,679 --> 00:01:42,396
A sampling profiler says a thread was in a socket read.

20
00:01:42,396 --> 00:01:47,463
JFR tells you which socket, to which address, for how long, with the stack.

21
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:51,791
Here's the configuration every service should have.

22
00:01:51,791 --> 00:02:00,998
Start a recording at launch with the default settings, keeping a rolling six-hour window on disk, capped at a gigabyte, and dump on exit.

23
00:02:00,998 --> 00:02:06,576
When an incident happens, JFR dot dump writes out the six hours before the command.

24
00:02:06,576 --> 00:02:08,122
That's the whole point.

25
00:02:08,122 --> 00:02:11,617
And dump on exit captures the window before a crash.

26
00:02:12,067 --> 00:02:15,161
The default profile costs about one percent.

27
00:02:15,161 --> 00:02:19,942
For an investigation, use the profile settings at about two percent.

28
00:02:19,942 --> 00:02:26,552
Note that only profile includes allocation events, so default can't answer what is allocating.

29
00:02:27,452 --> 00:02:29,109
Start with jfr summary.

30
00:02:29,109 --> 00:02:34,799
The event counts usually point at the problem before you look at anything else.

31
00:02:34,799 --> 00:02:39,698
Here, nearly nine thousand monitor contention events is the anomaly.

32
00:02:39,698 --> 00:02:45,244
And only sixty-one garbage collections in six hours rules GC out immediately.

33
00:02:45,694 --> 00:02:48,222
Then print those events with stacks.

34
00:02:48,222 --> 00:02:57,138
The monitor is the InventoryCache, the previous owner is named, each wait lasted nearly two seconds, and here's the exact line.

35
00:02:57,138 --> 00:03:02,895
A synchronized cache refresh was serialising every request thread during a reload.

36
00:03:02,895 --> 00:03:06,195
No reproduction, no added logging, no guessing.

37
00:03:07,095 --> 00:03:11,917
With the profile settings, allocation samples show what's allocating.

38
00:03:11,917 --> 00:03:15,131
Weight is the total allocated along that path.

39
00:03:15,131 --> 00:03:21,699
Here, an audit logger was serialising every order to a string even when audit logging was off.

40
00:03:21,699 --> 00:03:24,354
Four hundred megabytes in two minutes.

41
00:03:24,354 --> 00:03:26,381
The fix was one if statement.

42
00:03:26,831 --> 00:03:32,111
You can add your own domain events by extending jdk dot jfr dot Event.

43
00:03:32,111 --> 00:03:35,052
Begin, do the work, set fields, commit.

44
00:03:35,052 --> 00:03:41,388
Then your reservations appear on the same timeline as GC pauses and lock contention.

45
00:03:41,388 --> 00:03:47,951
When the event type is disabled, commit checks a flag and returns, so it's nearly free.

46
00:03:48,401 --> 00:03:54,004
And for virtual threads, the VirtualThreadPinned event finds what's pinning carriers.

47
00:03:54,004 --> 00:03:57,169
It supersedes the old trace pinned threads flag.

48
00:03:58,069 --> 00:04:04,938
JFR is the always-on baseline, low overhead and rich, but it only sees Java frames.

49
00:04:05,388 --> 00:04:15,955
async-profiler adds wall-clock profiling, meaning time spent blocked, not just on CPU, plus native frames and great flame graphs.

50
00:04:15,955 --> 00:04:18,167
It can even read JFR files.

51
00:04:18,617 --> 00:04:23,374
A thread dump is free and instant, but shows one moment with no history.

52
00:04:23,824 --> 00:04:30,459
A heap dump is the definitive answer for memory questions, but it's large and pauses the JVM.

53
00:04:30,909 --> 00:04:37,768
And APM agents trace requests across services, at higher overhead with less JVM detail.

54
00:04:37,768 --> 00:04:39,975
These complement each other.

55
00:04:40,875 --> 00:04:42,690
You don't need a GUI.

56
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:50,727
jfr summary first, then jfr print for specific events, with stacks, or as JSON piped into jq.

57
00:04:50,727 --> 00:04:56,171
And jcmd can start, dump and check recordings on a running JVM.

58
00:04:56,621 --> 00:04:59,072
Map your question to an event.

59
00:04:59,072 --> 00:05:04,056
CPU usage: execution samples, and Java 25's CPU time samples.

60
00:05:04,056 --> 00:05:07,487
Contention: monitor enter and thread park.

61
00:05:07,487 --> 00:05:10,265
Slow calls: socket and file reads.

62
00:05:10,265 --> 00:05:15,331
And exceptions thrown in a loop show up as JavaExceptionThrow.

63
00:05:16,231 --> 00:05:17,559
Production rules.

64
00:05:17,559 --> 00:05:22,948
Run continuous JFR on every service, with a max age and disk enabled.

65
00:05:22,948 --> 00:05:25,604
Use dump on exit to catch crashes.

66
00:05:25,604 --> 00:05:28,494
Run jfr summary before anything else.

67
00:05:28,494 --> 00:05:32,400
Use the profile settings for allocation questions.

68
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:36,540
And add custom events for your key domain operations.

69
00:05:36,990 --> 00:05:39,047
Never wait for a reproduction.

70
00:05:39,047 --> 00:05:42,817
Don't expect allocation data from the default settings.

71
00:05:42,817 --> 00:05:47,410
Don't use JFR for native-frame problems, which need async-profiler.

72
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:53,374
And don't write recordings to ephemeral container storage, which vanishes with the pod.

73
00:05:54,274 --> 00:05:54,954
To recap.

74
00:05:54,954 --> 00:05:59,865
JFR is built in and costs about one percent, so leave it running.

75
00:05:59,865 --> 00:06:05,154
A rolling window means that when something happens, you dump the past.

76
00:06:05,154 --> 00:06:09,084
Start with jfr summary, then drill into the anomaly.

77
00:06:09,084 --> 00:06:15,053
And custom events put your domain operations on the same timeline as the JVM's.

78
00:06:15,503 --> 00:06:18,391
Next is chapter thirty-eight: stream gatherers.

79
00:06:18,391 --> 00:06:19,620
Thanks for watching.
